# Canonical author identities for this repository.
#
# git, GitHub, `git shortlog -sne` and `git log --use-mailmap` all honour this
# file, so it corrects how authorship is DISPLAYED without rewriting a single
# commit object. That property is why it is the right instrument here: the
# tracked documentation carried 190 commit citations when this file was added
# (measured at 87bcbbb; the count grows with the history, so it is bound to a
# commit rather than left floating), verified on every pull request by the
# required `Cited commits resolve` check, and rewriting history to repair an
# author field would invalidate all of them to fix a display string.
#
# The mapping below is evidenced, not assumed. Nineteen commits made between
# 2026-08-16 and 2026-08-17 carry the author and committer identity
# `Test <test@example.com>`, left over from a git identity that was
# misconfigured for that two-day window. Every one of the nineteen is
# GPG-signed, and every signature verifies as good against the maintainer's
# key (`git log --format='%G? %GS'` reports `G` and
# `gavinmborges1104@gmail.com` for all nineteen). The signature is the
# authorship evidence; the author field is the artifact of the
# misconfiguration.
#
# Why no gate caught it: the DCO check verifies that a `Signed-off-by:`
# trailer MATCHES the commit author. These commits sign off as
# `Test <test@example.com>` and are authored by `Test <test@example.com>`, so
# they are internally consistent and DCO passes. Nothing in the pipeline
# asserts that the author is a person who exists. Consistency and correctness
# are different properties, and only the first one was being checked.
#
# Do NOT add a mapping here for a contributor who is genuinely a different
# person. This file exists to collapse duplicate identities belonging to one
# human, never to reassign someone else's work.

Gavin Borges <gavinmborges1104@gmail.com> <test@example.com>
